TRANSPILATION:
  ☐ Figure out TODOs
  ☐ BUG/GAP (found 2026-08-03 while writing equivalence tests): the standalone
    `transpile_source` path rejects two constructs the macro-sim accepts, with
    "cannot infer bit width; add an explicit type annotation":
      - `[Logic::Zero; N]` array locals (raw Logic arrays; `Bits::zero()` +
        indexed writes works instead)
      - tuple-returning plain-Rust helper fns called from hardware bodies
        (e.g. `let (s, c) = full_adder(...)`; inlining the expression works)
    Concretely, examples/combinational/ripple_carry_adder.rs and
    examples/basejump/bsg_gray_to_binary.rs (as written) do NOT round-trip through
    `transpile_source` / the copper-transpile CLI — they are only ever checked via
    the macro-sim + hand-written SV in their own main(). The new equivalence tests
    (tests/ripple_carry_adder_equivalence.rs, tests/gray_to_binary_equivalence.rs)
    use Bits-indexing rewrites of the same designs to get real sim≡SV coverage;
    the underlying transpiler inference gap is NOT fixed, just worked around.
  ☐ BUG (found 2026-08-03, writing the P2 six-state FSM equivalence test): a Moore
    output decoded from a single-register FSM is lowered into `always_ff`
    (REGISTERED, `out <= …`) instead of `always_comb` (a combinational decode of the
    state register). The transpiled output therefore lags the simulator by one cycle
    → `verilator: FAIL` while `trace: PASS` (sim is the source of truth, so this is a
    transpiler bug). A multi-bit `Out<Bits<N>>` output is ADDITIONALLY widened to 32
    bits (`code <= 32'd0`), a second defect on the same path.
      Repro: tests/fixtures/seq6_dut.rs (`seq6`) — an enum `st` ring whose Moore
      output is written via a statement `match st { … => out.write(const) }` before
      `clk.tick().await`. Empirically NOT triggered by Logic-vs-Bits outputs nor by a
      conditional-vs-unconditional transition (both ruled out). The differentiator vs
      the WORKING `traffic_light` (which correctly emits `always_comb` outputs) is
      that traffic_light has a SECOND state register (`timer`) and its output decodes
      only `phase`; `seq6`'s lone `st` is both the sole register and the output
      source, and the output writes get fused into its `always_ff`.
      Fix later (a CODEGEN change — get sign-off first): emit the pre-tick Moore
      output decode in `always_comb` from the current state register, as the
      traffic_light path already does, regardless of register count. Meanwhile
      tests/fsm_six_state_equivalence.rs runs `sim_only` (sim≡reference verified;
      sim≡SV blocked on this bug) — flip it to a full equivalence test once fixed.

